☁️ 2. vertualization
Virtualization is the process of dividing one physical machine into multiple virtual machines using a Hypervisor.

> Why Do We Need Virtualization?
> Before virtualization:
One physical server ran only one operating system.
Hardware resources were underutilized.
Higher hardware, power, and maintenance costs.

> After virtualization:
One physical server can run multiple virtual machines.
Better resource utilization.
Lower cost.
> How Virtualization Works
The Hypervisor creates and manages virtual machines by sharing the physical hardware resources.
> Example
Suppose you have a server with:
CPU: 16 Cores
RAM: 64 GB
Storage: 2 TB
Without virtualization:
Only one operating system uses the entire server.
With virtualization:
VM1 → Ubuntu (4 CPU, 16 GB RAM)

> Types of Virtualization
1. Server Virtualization
Creates multiple virtual servers from one physical server.
Example:
Running Ubuntu, Windows Server, and CentOS on one physical machine.
2. Desktop Virtualization
Runs desktop operating systems virtually.
Example:
Using Windows Virtual Desktop from the cloud.
3. Storage Virtualization
Combines multiple storage devices into one logical storage pool.
Example:
AWS EBS, SAN Storage.
4. Network Virtualization
Creates virtual networks independent of physical hardware.
Example:
AWS VPC.
5. Application Virtualization
Runs applications without installing them directly on the operating system.
Example:
Microsoft App-V.

> Advantages of Virtualization
Better hardware utilization
Cost savings
High availability
Easy backup and recovery
> Disadvantages of Virtualization
Performance overhead compared to bare metal
Requires powerful hardware
Hypervisor failure can affect multiple VMs

> Real-World Example
A company has 10 physical servers, each running one application. Most servers use only 20–30% of their CPU and memory.
By using virtualization:
They consolidate all workloads onto 2–3 physical servers.
Each application runs in its own VM.

