☁️ Virtual Machine (VM)
A Virtual Machine (VM) is a software-based computer that behaves like a real physical computer. Each VM has its own:
Operating System
CPU allocation
RAM allocation
Storage
Network

Q why we need Virtual Machine.
A Virtual Machine (VM) is a software-based computer that runs on a physical computer. It allows us to run multiple operating systems on a single physical machine.

Why do we need a VM?
1 Run multiple operating systems
Example: Run Windows and Linux on the same computer.
2 Better resource utilization
One physical server can host many virtual machines, reducing hardware costs.
3 Isolation
If one VM crashes or is infected with malware, other VMs are not affected.
Testing and development
4 Developers can safely test applications or new operating systems without affecting the host system.
5 Easy backup and recovery
VMs can be cloned, snapshotted, and restored quickly.

Q.  Hypervisor and type.
A Hypervisor is software that creates, runs, and manages Virtual Machines (VMs). It allocates CPU, memory, storage, and networking resources to each VM.
Without a Hypervisor, virtualization is not possible.

> Type 1 Hypervisor (Bare Metal)
Installed directly on the physical hardware.
Examples:
VMware ESXi
Microsoft Hyper-V
Xen
KVM
Advantages
High performance
Better Security
> Type 2 Hypervisor (Hosted)
Installed on top of an existing operating system.
Examples:
Oracle VirtualBox
VMware Workstation
VMware Fusion
Advantages
Easy to install
Good for learning and testing

Q.  What is the difference between Virtualization and Cloud Computing?
Virtualization is a technology that creates virtual machines on physical hardware.
Cloud Computing delivers computing resources (servers, storage, networking, databases, etc.) over the internet, often using virtualization behind the scenes.

Q.  Why is Virtualization important in Cloud Computing?
Virtualization allows cloud providers like AWS to run many virtual servers on a single physical server. This improves resource utilization, enables scalability, reduces costs, and allows customers to create or remove virtual machines on demand.

Q.  how Hypervisor works.
A Hypervisor is software that sits between the physical hardware and the Virtual Machines (VMs). It creates, manages, and allocates hardware resources such as CPU, RAM, storage, and network to each VM.
Working Process: 
The physical server provides CPU, RAM, storage, and network resources.
The Hypervisor is installed on the server.
The Hypervisor creates multiple Virtual Machines (VMs)

Q.  What are the benefits of Virtual Machines (VMs)?
Answer
Virtual Machines provide several advantages:
1. Better Resource Utilization
Multiple VMs share one physical server, reducing idle hardware.
2. Cost Savings
Fewer physical servers mean lower hardware, electricity, cooling, and maintenance costs.
3. Isolation
Each VM is independent. If one VM fails or is compromised, others are not affected.
4. Scalability
New VMs can be created or removed quickly based on business needs.
5. Multiple Operating Systems
Run Windows, Linux, Ubuntu, and CentOS simultaneously on the same physical machine.
6. Easy Backup and Recovery
VMs can be backed up using snapshots and restored quickly.
7. Faster Deployment
Creating a VM takes only a few minutes compared to setting up a new physical server.

Q. why do companies use virtualization.
Companies use virtualization to improve efficiency, reduce costs, and simplify infrastructure management.
> Reasons:
Reduce Infrastructure Costs by running multiple VMs on one physical server.
Improve Resource Utilization so CPU and memory are used efficiently.
Increase Flexibility by creating, deleting, or modifying VMs quickly.
Support Business Growth through easy scaling.




